<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="advance-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="advance-title">Fees received before teaching</h2>
      <p>Under section 13 of the CGST Act, the time of supply of a service is generally the earlier of the invoice date and the date payment is received, where the invoice is issued on time. So for a registered institute, a fee paid at enrolment can bring tax due before the first class.</p>
      <p class="para">Section 31(3)(d) asks a registered person to issue a receipt voucher, or another document, on receiving an advance. Rule 50 lists what it should show, including the amount of advance and the tax charged. Ask your accountant how your fee receipts should be set up.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Is coaching exempt from GST?</h2>
      <p>Not simply because it is teaching. The exemption for education turns on the notification's definition of an educational institution, which is tied to education leading to a qualification recognised by law. In 2022 the Andhra Pradesh appellate authority held that a CA and CMA coaching institute without recognition from the professional institutes did not qualify.</p>
      <p class="para">An advance ruling binds only the applicant, and your facts may differ. Ask whether you need to register, and how your courses are treated.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="refund-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="refund-title">Refunds when a student leaves</h2>
      <p>In January 2024 the Ministry of Education issued guidelines for regulating coaching centres, as a model for states and union territories to adopt. As reported, they ask for fee receipts, no fee increase during a course, and a pro-rata refund within 10 days if a student who paid in full leaves midway, including hostel and mess fees where the centre provides them.</p>
      <p class="para">We have not found that Delhi has adopted these as law. Even so, keep refunds traceable. Section 31(3)(e) allows a refund voucher where an advance was received but no supply was made. For part of a course already taught, ask what the right document is.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Faculty, rent and cash</h2>
      <p>If you pay visiting faculty per lecture or as a share of fees, keep a written agreement and a monthly statement for each teacher, and ask whether tax must be deducted on these payments.</p>
      <p class="para">If your classrooms are rented, keep the lease and rent receipts. Fees paid in cash or UPI should agree with the student register each day. See {% routeLink "/services/tds-return-filing/", "TDS return filing" %} and {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-small-businesses/", "accounting for small businesses" %}.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="ask-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="ask-title">What to ask an accountant</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Have you worked with coaching institutes that take full fees upfront?</li>
        <li>How should we record a refund for a student who leaves halfway?</li>
        <li>Is our study material part of the course fee for GST, or separate?</li>
        <li>How do we split fees for a batch that runs past 31 March?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>What we can say about this area</h2>
      <p>AccountOrbit has no office in Laxmi Nagar, and takes enquiries by phone from anywhere in Delhi. This page explains the area's main trade. Much of this work can be done remotely if your student and fee records are digital. See {% routeLink "/locations/", "whether your accountant needs to be nearby" %}.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

